# Fusing Unitree L2 LiDAR + Camera Into a Walkable, Relightable UE5 Environment: 2026 State of the Art and a Recommended Pipeline

## TL;DR
- **The strongest 2026 path is a hybrid: use RealityScan (free RealityCapture) to jointly reconstruct one mesh from BOTH your 2,200 photos and the Unitree L2 point cloud, letting photogrammetry supply fine detail/texture and the LiDAR act as the metric-scale-and-structure anchor on flat/low-texture regions — then UV-unwrap, bake, de-light to albedo, and export to UE5 as a Nanite mesh with PBR materials.** This keeps genuine camera+LiDAR fusion as your edge while producing a walkable, relightable asset.
- **Your instinct to keep "LiDAR geometry authoritative + camera texture only" is partly wrong for your specific hardware.** The Unitree L2 is a ±2 cm robotics-grade SLAM LiDAR; with 2,200 high-res posed photos, photogrammetry will usually be denser and at least as metrically accurate on textured surfaces. The evidence supports LiDAR-for-structure + photogrammetry-for-detail, not LiDAR-as-master. Your custom projection tool is a sound *texturing* method but the wrong place to make geometry decisions.
- **Gaussian splatting (even LiDAR-guided) is the wrong final format for you.** It gives fast photoreal capture but does not natively relight (splats bake lighting) and is not truly walkable/editable in UE5 without conversion to mesh. Use splatting only as an optional intermediate; deliver a de-lit, UV-textured mesh for day-to-night previz.

## Key Findings

1. **Three real fusion families exist.** (a) *LiDAR-geometry + photo-projection texturing* (your tool): geometry from LiDAR, color projected from posed images. (b) *Photogrammetry-with-LiDAR-prior*: photos build/refine geometry while LiDAR constrains depth/scale (RealityScan, Metashape, LiDAR-guided NeRF). (c) *Tightly-coupled joint optimization*: both sensors jointly optimize geometry AND appearance (research: joint BA+cloud registration; neural SiLVR, GS-SDF).

2. **For a photoreal + accurate result, tightly-coupled or photogrammetry-with-LiDAR-prior wins over pure LiDAR-mesh-plus-projection.** Pure LiDAR mesh + projection is limited by the LiDAR's own geometric fidelity and its sparse/uneven sampling — exactly the weakness of the Unitree L2.

3. **The Unitree L2 is navigation-grade, not survey-grade.** Vendor spec (Unitree 4D LiDAR L2 User Manual 2024.10 v1.1 and unitree.com/mobile/L2): measurement accuracy ≤2.0 cm, 4.5 mm distance resolution, 64,000 effective points/s (sampling up to ~128,000 pts/s), 0.05–30 m range (30 m at 90% reflectivity), 360°×96° FOV in negative-angle mode, 230 g, built-in 3-axis IMU. By contrast the Leica RTC360 has 1.0 mm + 10 ppm range accuracy and 3D point accuracy of 1.9 mm at 10 m (2.9 mm at 20 m, 5.3 mm at 40 m) at up to 2 million pts/s (Leica Geosystems RTC360 datasheet) — roughly an order of magnitude more accurate per point. SLAM adds decimeter-scale drift over a full scene; users report loop-closure problems with Unitree's official Point-LIO stack.

4. **RealityScan (formerly RealityCapture) genuinely fuses both and is free for you.** It ingests images + LiDAR/SLAM data into one reconstruction, and has explicit controls to prioritize LiDAR geometry where you want it. RealityScan is free for students, educators and companies under US $1 million annual gross revenue; above that a commercial licence is US $1,250/seat/year (realityscan.com/license, confirmed in CG Channel's June 2026 RealityScan 2.2 coverage). This is the pragmatic professional tool.

5. **LiDAR-guided NeRF and LiDAR-informed Gaussian splatting are real and hot in 2025-2026, but neither natively delivers relightable + walkable + editable UE5 assets** without a mesh-extraction + de-lighting step.

6. **Relightability requires de-lit albedo.** Whatever path builds geometry, you must UV-unwrap, bake a texture atlas, and remove baked lighting/shadows (de-light) to get an albedo the UE5 lighting can drive day-to-night.

## Details

### 1. Fusion methods for LiDAR + camera 3D reconstruction

**(a) LiDAR mesh + photo-projection texturing (your current approach).** Build a mesh from the LiDAR point cloud (Poisson/ball-pivoting surface reconstruction), then project the posed camera images onto the mesh, selecting the best image per face by the angle between face normal and camera view vector (near-perpendicular = highest resolution, fewest artifacts). This "best-view-per-face" selection is a well-established, professional texturing technique (documented in reconstruction literature and patents; the same logic drives MeshLab "Transfer Vertex Attributes to Texture" and Blender camera-projection baking). **It is sound as a texturing method.** Its weakness is that final geometry quality is entirely capped by the LiDAR, and your tool only renders 2D output — it does not yet emit a UV-unwrapped, atlas-baked mesh.

To make it output a proper relightable mesh you need: (1) UV unwrapping (xatlas, Blender Smart UV Project, or RealityScan's unwrap), (2) texture-atlas baking (project best-view color per texel into the atlas, blend across views weighted by view angle, inpaint occluded holes), (3) de-lighting to albedo, and (4) export FBX/glTF + textures to UE5.

**(b) Photogrammetry with LiDAR prior.** Photos build dense geometry via multi-view stereo; LiDAR supplies metric scale and constrains depth on low-texture regions where photogrammetry fails. This is what RealityScan and Metashape do. Metashape note: it builds meshes from its own depth maps, not directly from imported LiDAR, but LiDAR can be merged into the dense cloud and used to guide/validate. RealityScan more genuinely meshes from mixed inputs and lets you bias toward LiDAR.

**(c) Tightly-coupled joint optimization.** Research systems jointly solve bundle adjustment + cloud registration to co-optimize camera poses, extrinsics, geometry, and appearance. The classic offline method (joint BA + registration, arXiv:1907.00930) reports 2.7 mm accuracy and 70 pts/cm². Modern neural versions (SiLVR, GS-SDF) fuse both in a radiance/SDF field. Highest quality, highest complexity.

### 2. Neural / modern fusion (2024-2026)

**LiDAR-guided NeRF.** LiDeNeRF projects the LiDAR cloud to sparse depth maps, densifies via multi-view propagation, and adds a depth-correction module for high geometric accuracy + photoreal rendering. LidaRF/DiL-NeRF (CVPR 2024) accumulate LiDAR frames for occlusion-aware depth supervision on street scenes. **SiLVR — by Yifu Tao & Maurice Fallon, IEEE Transactions on Robotics 2025 (arXiv:2502.02657)** — is the most directly relevant: a NeRF that "fuses lidar and vision data to generate high-quality reconstructions that are geometrically accurate and capture photorealistic texture," adding "strong geometric constraints on the depth and surface normals, which is particularly useful when modeling uniform texture surfaces." It uses a LiDAR-SLAM trajectory to bootstrap SfM and scales via submapping — demonstrated on handheld/legged-robot/drone captures, with public code. NeRF outputs, however, need mesh extraction and de-lighting to become walkable, relightable UE5 assets.

**LiDAR-informed Gaussian splatting.** This is the hottest 2025-2026 area. LiDAR provides metric geometric priors that fix 3DGS's scale ambiguity, floaters, and view-dependence: LiDAR-GS/Li-GS, GS-SDF (IROS 2025, HKU MARS — unifies 3DGS with a neural SDF for geometrically consistent rendering AND surface reconstruction), GeomGS, GTLR-GS (2026, LiDAR-centric allocation), DiffLiGS (2026, initializes 3DGS from voxel-downsampled LiDAR + dense depth supervision), and Structured-Li-GS (2026, anchors Gaussians to sub-sampled LiDAR, uses a custom hardware-synced LiDAR-camera handheld scanner). **BUT: relightability is a separate, hard problem.** Vanilla splats bake lighting and do not relight. A distinct research line (Relightable 3D Gaussians, GS-IR, Ref-Gaussian ICLR 2025, SVG-IR CVPR 2025, GaRe, RTR-GS) does inverse rendering to decompose BRDF + lighting, enabling relighting — but these are research-grade, not production tools, and LiDAR-fusion does not by itself make splats relightable. **Splat-to-mesh** is mature enough to be useful: SuGaR — by Antoine Guédon & Vincent Lepetit (CVPR 2024, pp. 5354–5363) — aligns Gaussians to the surface and Poisson-extracts an editable textured mesh "within minutes... compared to hours with the state-of-the-art method on neural SDFs" (full optimization 20–45 min per the official Anttwo/SuGaR GitHub), with a Blender add-on; Gaussian Opacity Fields, 2DGS, and 3DGS-to-PC all also convert splats to meshes.

**NVIDIA Omniverse NuRec** ingests real camera/LiDAR sensor data, reconstructs via 3DGS/3DGUT, and packages a USD/USDZ scene; the companion stereo-camera reference workflow uses nvblox to also generate a triangle mesh + occupancy map, and a "Fixer" diffusion model cleans reconstruction artifacts. It is aimed at autonomous-vehicle/robotics simulation (Isaac Sim, CARLA), not film previz, and it is splat-centric.

### 3. Tools that actually do LiDAR+camera fusion (2026)

- **RealityScan 2.x (Epic, formerly RealityCapture).** Genuinely combines images + laser/SLAM point clouds into one reconstruction; automatic registration, coloring, texturing, meshing of LiDAR; can reconstruct areas invisible to LiDAR using photos. Explicit reconstruction settings ("Default grouping factor," "Adaptive blending start") let you prioritize laser-scan geometry over image geometry when both are present. 2.1 added SLAM (trajectory + images + point cloud) import; 2.2 added full AMD GPU support. **Free** for individuals/companies under $1M annual revenue (US $1,250/seat/year above that); requires Windows + a CUDA-capable NVIDIA GPU. Exports meshes + textures directly into Unreal. This is the single most practical fusion tool for you.
- **Agisoft Metashape.** Co-processes images + LiDAR; builds meshes from its own depth maps (not directly from LiDAR), merges LiDAR into the dense cloud, has native de-lighting (Agisoft De-Lighter). Paid (Standard ~affordable; Pro higher). Note: it does not process raw .las/.laz for depth-map generation — LiDAR is imported as a point cloud to align/merge/validate.
- **NVIDIA Omniverse NuRec / Isaac Sim.** Neural (splat) reconstruction from camera+LiDAR to USD; robotics/AV-focused; free SDK but heavy and not previz-oriented.
- **Survey/mapping (Leica RTC360/BLK, NavVis VLX, Faro).** Output survey-grade colorized point clouds (2-4 mm) and can feed meshing, but the hardware is far above your budget; they confirm the professional "LiDAR structure + camera color" division. VFX houses (e.g., Visualskies) explicitly combine Leica RTC360/P50 LiDAR + photogrammetry in RealityCapture for film environments, and are now adding Gaussian splatting for fast previz.
- **Open source.** SiLVR (LiDAR-visual NeRF, mesh via Poisson), GS-SDF, SuGaR + nerfstudio/gsplat, Open3D/MeshLab (Poisson meshing + texture transfer), COLMAP (SfM/MVS; can be seeded with LiDAR depth), xatlas (UV). A viable free stack, but you assemble it yourself.

### 4. Your existing approach: validate or improve

Your custom "LiDAR mesh + per-face best-image projection" is a legitimate professional texturing method — but two corrections:

- **Output a mesh, not a 2D render.** Add UV unwrap → atlas bake → de-light → FBX/glTF export. Without this you can never get a walkable, relightable UE5 asset.
- **Reconsider "LiDAR geometry authoritative."** For your hardware this is the weak link. The verified evidence: a peer-reviewed iPad-LiDAR (±1-1.5 cm, same accuracy class as your L2) vs photogrammetry study (MDPI Sensors) found up to 50% of the LiDAR points unaligned on small complex geometry due to sparse sampling, while photogrammetry produced the densest, sharpest models; and Pix4D's mobile workflow actually inverts the roles — using photogrammetry to CORRECT mobile-LiDAR GPS/IMU drift, treating the photo solution as the more reliable geometric anchor. Conversely, the same literature shows ±2 cm LiDAR is genuinely better on large flat/low-texture surfaces (walls, floors) where photogrammetry starves for features, and it nails absolute scale. Peer-reviewed fusion work (MDPI Remote Sensing 17(3):443, 2025) states the standard practice plainly: "LiDAR provided the structural geometry, while photogrammetry added surface [detail]."

**Verdict:** With 2,200 posed photos, letting photogrammetry (in RealityScan) build the fine geometry and using the L2 to anchor scale + fill low-texture/flat/shadowed regions will give a better walkable environment than forcing the L2 to be the master geometry. Keep your fusion philosophy — both sensors contribute — but change WHICH sensor leads WHERE. That IS the professional fusion your edge should be built on.

### 5. Walkable + relightable specifically

- **Walkable = full coverage without holes.** A single vantage (even 360° LiDAR) leaves occlusion shadows behind every object. You must capture from multiple walked positions with overlapping views, and your reconstruction must fuse them. Photogrammetry + LiDAR fusion (RealityScan) handles multi-position merge natively; RealityScan's coverage heatmaps flag gaps before meshing. Fill remaining holes with mesh hole-filling or inpainting.
- **Relightable = de-lit albedo + PBR maps.** The texture baked from photos contains sun/shadow. To relight day-to-night you must remove it: Unity De-Lighting Tool, Agisoft De-Lighter, or manual high-pass techniques, producing a neutral albedo; then author roughness/metallic; then in UE5 build a PBR material and let Lumen light it. This is a documented, mature UE5/Unity photogrammetry workflow (Sketchfab, Unreal Kite-demo tutorials). Newer 2025 intrinsic-decomposition/diffusion de-lighters can help but verify quality. Splat inverse-rendering can also yield albedo but is research-grade.

## Recommendations

**Stage 1 — Prove the fused reconstruction (days, free).** Put all 2,200 photos + the L2 point cloud into **RealityScan** on your cloud RTX workstation. Register them together (use the L2 for scale/structure). Generate one mesh, letting photos drive detail and LiDAR stabilize flat/low-texture areas via the grouping/adaptive-blending settings. Inspect coverage heatmaps; re-capture gaps if needed. *Benchmark that changes the plan:* if photogrammetry alone (photos only) reconstructs the flat/low-texture zones cleanly, the L2's contribution is marginal; if those zones are noisy/holey without LiDAR, the fusion is earning its keep.

**Stage 2 — Make it walkable + game-ready.** Clean/retopo or rely on UE5 Nanite for the high-poly mesh; UV-unwrap (RealityScan or xatlas); bake an 8K/16K texture atlas.

**Stage 3 — Make it relightable.** De-light the albedo (Agisoft De-Lighter or Unity De-Lighting Tool), author roughness/metallic, import to UE5, build a PBR material, and test a day-to-night Lumen lighting sweep.

**Stage 4 — Keep and sharpen YOUR edge.** Retain your best-view-per-face projector as a *texturing refinement* on top of the fused mesh (it can beat generic texturers on sharpness by choosing optimal source images per face). Extend it to write a UV atlas + de-lit albedo. This is where your differentiation genuinely lives — superior fusion-driven texturing on metrically-anchored geometry — not in forcing LiDAR to be the sole geometry source.

**Optional parallel track — evaluate LiDAR-guided splatting for speed/quality.** If capture-to-preview speed matters, try a LiDAR-informed 3DGS (e.g., GS-SDF or a nerfstudio/gsplat pipeline seeded with the L2 cloud), then SuGaR/GOF to extract a mesh, then de-light. Treat this as R&D, not the deliverable, until relightable-splat tooling matures.

**Thresholds that change the recommendation:** (a) If you upgrade to a survey-grade scanner (~mm accuracy, e.g., RTC360 at 1.9 mm @ 10 m), flip back to LiDAR-authoritative geometry + photo texture. (b) If the client only needs a fly-through (not editable relit sets), a LiDAR-guided splat may suffice and skip meshing. (c) If revenue exceeds $1M, RealityScan requires a US $1,250/seat/year subscription (or a $1,850/seat/year bundle with Unreal, per flypix.ai and realityscan.com/license) — factor licensing.

## Caveats
- **No independent accuracy benchmark exists for the Unitree L2 specifically**; the ≤2 cm figure is vendor spec, and my quality assessment leans on Livox/iPad-LiDAR proxies of the same accuracy class. Verify on your own scenes.
- **Relightable Gaussian splatting is research-grade**, not production; do not promise day-to-night relighting from a splat pipeline yet.
- **RealityScan meshes from photo depth maps**; the degree to which LiDAR "wins" geometry depends on your settings and scene — validate visually.
- Metashape does not mesh directly from imported LiDAR; it merges LiDAR into the dense cloud.
- De-lighting quality varies with capture lighting; overcast/diffuse capture de-lights far better than harsh sun.
- UE5 Gaussian-splat support is via third-party plugins (XVERSE XScene, MLSLabs, and others), not a first-party Epic module as of 2026; splats also don't respond to dynamic Lumen lights.
- SLAM-based mobile LiDAR (including the L2) accumulates trajectory drift; loop closures and multi-position registration quality materially affect final geometry, and Unitree's own Point-LIO stack has documented loop-closure issues.